{
 "what_this_file_is": "Final acceptance numbers for the gate-shaped contact compliance AFTER the verifier findings (ADMM step size poisoned by c0; AC3 graded on a truncation artifact; c0 unbounded below; the law reachable from no entry point) were reproduced and fixed. Every number is read out of the JSON produced by the driver named next to it; nothing here is transcribed by hand. All source md5s were identical before and after every measurement (each driver asserts that itself and records md5_stable).",
 "change": {
  "law": "R = [c0 (1 - s^2) + reg] * scale * I_per_contact in _constraint_contact_solve, c0 = floor + softplus(_contact_compliance_raw), floor = _CONTACT_COMPLIANCE_FLOOR_FRACTION (0.1) * the initial c0 the constructor was given. Default disabled (contact_compliance=None).",
  "admm_step_size": "rho is the mean of the gated Delassus diagonal plus the conditioning floor, diag(W) + reg * scale, not the mean of diag(H). The two are elementwise identical when R = reg * scale * I, so the disabled branch keeps the literal diag(H) expression (a mean over a strided diagonal view does not round like a mean over a contiguous vector, and the single-expression version broke bit-exactness on 8 of 25 fresh models); the enabled branch is c0-independent.",
  "entry_points": "dynamics_contact_compliance in the benchmark CSV namespace (popped by benchmarks/run_ct_rl.py:_pop_structured_model_kwargs) and --contact_compliance in evaluations/hamiltonian_recovery.py, threaded into every PortHamiltonianModel it builds. A number is c0 itself, 'true' takes the model default, blank/'false' leaves the law off.",
  "refused": "contact_compliance with contact_solver='compliant', with contact_force=0, and with c0 <= 0 / nan / inf."
 },
